I don't think this works. For one, you cannot modify the container: 
https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/blob/26c3f66887d13efc8cd627b50beeeef060c2e39c/pkg/apis/core/validation/validation.go#L4936. I'm not sure why it does not fail here, but I don't see it updated when I fetch the pod afterwards. Also, this fails: 
```golang
	pod0.Spec.Containers[0].Lifecycle = &v1.Lifecycle{
		PreStop: &v1.LifecycleHandler{
			Exec: &v1.ExecAction{
				Command: []string{"/bin/sh", "-c", "sleep 500"},
			},
		},
	}
	pod0, err = c.CoreV1().Pods(ns).Update(ctx, pod0, metav1.UpdateOptions{})
```

Second, I think the issue isn't with delaying the deletion, either with graceful termination, or finalizers. I think the issue occurs when the pod is deleted completely, so the `status.Replicas` is 2, as the field counts any existing pods: https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/blob/master/pkg/controller/statefulset/stateful_set_control.go#L324-L326.

The current version of the test deletes the pod0 from the code test, and this is why it fails. However, it does not fail consistently, as the stateful set controller may recreate the pod before checking the `completeRollingUpdate` condition. So, the longer the pod is deleted and not recreated, the the test would fail (if not for the fix). However, I'm not quite sure  we can delay the pod recreation in e2e test to make it fail 100% times.

I would suggest keeping the e2e test, but also adding an integration test, Here we can be triggering the STS reconciliations on demand, to ensure `completeRollingUpdate` is called before the pod is recreated.

Finally, the issue with the `completeRollingUpdate` is coincidentally meaning that since https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/pull/115331, we have a bug exposed in https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/pull/120398. The same fix will work. However, given the complicated structure of dependencies between bugs I suggest we put effort to write down good tests, following the sentiment of https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/pull/120731#issuecomment-1737323262. In particular, I would suggest that we also add an integration test as part of re-introducing https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/pull/120398 (for the scenario there). @aleksandra-malinowska wdyt? I can help with the integration test.

EDIT: actually, on the second thought, I suppose e2e test fails currently consistently, because the deleted pod0 ends with `Succeeded` phase, which is not recreated by STS controller on master currently, as the fix for recreating `Succeeded` pods was reverted. So,  it will stop being consistent once we re-introduce. This emphasizes also the need for an integration test IMO.

